            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Impact on Technology</strong><br>\nThe invention of the transistor marked a significant milestone in technology. It led to the miniaturization of electronic circuits and was vital in the creation of smaller, more portable electronic devices. Brattain's work contributed to the development of the information age, influencing everything from computers to cellular phones.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Recognition</strong><br>\nFor their groundbreaking work on the transistor, Brattain, along with Bardeen and Shockley, was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1956. The prize recognized their contributions to semiconductor technology and the foundation they provided for the electronics industry. Brattain's Nobel Prize is a testament to the profound impact of his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Career and Teaching</strong><br>\nAfter the invention of the transistor, Brattain continued his research at Bell Laboratories. He focused on understanding the surface properties of semiconductors and exploring the physical processes involved in semiconductor behavior. He also spent time teaching and mentoring young scientists, sharing his knowledge and experience.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
