          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Interests</strong><br>\nOutside his scientific work, Brattain led a fulfilling personal life. He was known for his love of the outdoors, often taking time to go hiking and skiing. He balanced his career with a rich family life and a variety of hobbies, showing that he valued both his professional and personal endeavors.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Memberships and Honors</strong><br>\nBrattain held memberships in several scientific organizations, including the National Academy of Sciences. He received numerous awards and honors throughout his career, in addition to the Nobel Prize. These honors reflect the high esteem in which the scientific community held his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nWalter Brattain passed away on October 13, 1987. His legacy, however, lives on in the countless electronic devices that utilize transistors. His contribution to the field of physics and engineering remains invaluable, and his name is enshrined among the great scientists who have shaped the modern world.</p>\n"
              },
